   TRIVIAL as a pooled count: every odd u <= X is a node, so this is #{odd u = 1 mod 6 <= X} / #{odd u, 3 !| u, u <= X};
   only the depth-by-depth rows above carry tree information.
largest deviation of a mod-9 fraction from 1/9 over depths with >= 1000 nodes: 0.0194 at depth 91
Fibre-uniformity lemma (PROVED): for any internal u, n_j mod 9 depends on 4^j mod 27; ord_27(4) = 9 and
   <4> mod 27 = [1, 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25] = all residues 1 mod 3; so (2^(h0+1) 4^j u - 1)/3 runs over EVERY residue mod 9 exactly once
   per 9 consecutive j.  Hence the infinite fibre of every internal node is uniform mod 9: 1/3 of the children
   are row-3 leaves, 1/6 lie in each of the six classes 1,2,4,5,7,8 mod 9 -- NOT the greedy law 2/9, 1/9.
   checked for u in {1,5,7,11,13,17,19,23,25,29}: children j=0..8 hit each residue mod 9 once.
FINITE-EXACT: #{odd n <= X : T(n) <= X} = sum over internal u <= X of (J_u(X)+1) = 416666, J_u(X) = floor(log_4((3X+1)/(2^(h0+1)u)));
   333333 internal u <= X, mean truncated fibre size 1.2500 (the infinite fibre is infinite; truncation keeps the first
   J_u(X)+1 children, so small u contribute the phase-dependent prefix of the 9-periodic residue cycle).
depth-2 anatomy: 34 nodes = 33 children of the six internal depth-1 nodes <= X (parents {5: 10, 85: 7, 341: 7, 5461: 4, 21845: 4, 349525: 1})
   plus 932067, whose parent 1398101 exceeds X.  So the finite level sets are not fibre truncations alone.
Discrepancy with G explained: (i) G never visits multiples of 3 (its k skips row-3 children) while the tree
   counts every leaf once (1/3 of nodes); (ii) among internal children the infinite tree is uniform on the six classes
   (lemma), whereas G's greedy minimal k lands in 1 mod 3 from residues {1,2,4,5} and in 2 mod 3 only from {7,8},
   giving 2/3 on 1 mod 3 (E-graph lane Theorem 4.2).  HEURISTIC, not proved: that the finite depth-by-depth deviations
   from uniform come only from the phase-dependent prefixes of truncated fibres (and from parents above X).

==============================================================================
S3  Growth: branching factor 4/3, (4/3)^k exact average, depth-k counts, modulus-9 difference inequality, literature
==============================================================================
pruned T-tree (nodes: integers with 3 !| n; children 2y always, (2y-1)/3 iff y = 2 or 8 mod 9):
   children count by y mod 9: {1: 1, 2: 2, 4: 1, 5: 1, 7: 1, 8: 2}; mean over the six classes = 4/3
   unpruned (multiples of 3 kept as nodes): y = 2 mod 3 always has the odd child, mean 3/2
PROVED: a node uniform on the six classes mod 9 has 4/3 children on average in the pruned tree (3/2 unpruned).
   k | 3^(k+1) | sum over root classes of #depth-k nodes | average | (4/3)^k
   1 |      9 |        8 | 4/3 | 4/3
   2 |     27 |       32 | 16/9 | 16/9
   3 |     81 |      128 | 64/27 | 64/27
   4 |    243 |      512 | 256/81 | 256/81
   5 |    729 |     2048 | 1024/243 | 1024/243
   6 |   2187 |     8192 | 4096/729 | 4096/729
   7 |   6561 |    32768 | 16384/2187 | 16384/2187
PROVED: the child of a node uniform on the six classes mod 3^(s+1) is uniform on the six classes mod 3^s
   (doubling permutes classes; (2y-1)/3 sends 9m+2 -> 6m+1 and 9m+8 -> 6m+5, each uniform in its mod-3 class),
   so the expected number of depth-k descendants of a uniformly random root is exactly (4/3)^k (FINITE-EXACT k<=7).
   No novelty claim: this is the Applegate-Lagarias/Lagarias-Weiss branching model (CITED below).
FINITE-EXACT: pruned T-tree from root 8, nodes at depth k = 0..32:
   [1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4, 6, 10, 13, 14, 18, 25, 33, 46, 61, 77, 107, 144, 189, 253, 331, 441, 591, 802, 1066, 1412, 1876, 2492, 3345, 4453, 5936, 7925, 10563]
   ratios count[k]/count[k-1] for k = 24..32: ['1.329', '1.325', '1.329', '1.328', '1.342', '1.331', '1.333', '1.335', '1.333'];  (count[32])^(1/32) = 1.3358 vs 4/3 = 1.3333
Modulus-9 difference inequality (PROVED).  Let N = {n >= 1 : 3 !| n, n reaches 1 under T}, f_r(x) = #{n in N, n <= x, n = r mod 9},
   A = f_1+f_4+f_7 (1 mod 3), B = f_2+f_5+f_8 (2 mod 3).  Children are distinct (T is a function), so for x >= 1:
   (i)  A(x) >= B(x/2) + f_2(3x/2),   B(x) >= A(x/2) + f_8(3x/2)   [doubling swaps the mod-3 class; odd children of
        n = 2 mod 9 are 1 mod 3 and <= x iff n <= (3x+1)/2; of n = 8 mod 9 are 2 mod 3];
   (ii) doubling chains 1->2 (1 step), 7->5->1->2 (3), 4->8->7->5->1->2 (5) give f_2(y) >= max(f_1(y/2), f_7(y/8), f_4(y/32)) >= A(y/32)/3;
        4->8 (1), 1->2->4->8 (3), 7->5->1->2->4->8 (5) give f_8(y) >= A(y/32)/3.
   doubling chain lengths (r -> target): {(1, 2): 1, (1, 8): 3, (4, 2): 5, (4, 8): 1, (7, 2): 3, (7, 8): 5}
   (iii) hence A(x) >= A(x/4) + A(3x/128)/3 + A(3x/64)/3 for all x >= 1, and A(x) >= 1 for x >= 1 (1 in N).
   Induction on x with A nondecreasing gives A(x) >= c x^gamma where gamma solves 1 = 4^-g + (1/3)(3/128)^g + (1/3)(3/64)^g:
   gamma_9,crude = 0.246227  (so #{n <= x : n reaches 1} >= c x^0.2462; a weak but rigorous power bound)
Adversarial constant-split modulus-9 system (HEURISTIC): f_s(x) = sum_{2r=s} f_r(x/2) + sum_{r in {2,8}} alpha_{r->s} f_r(3x/2),
   alpha_{2->.} a distribution on {1,4,7}, alpha_{8->.} on {2,5,8} (the unknown splits of the odd children by n mod 27).
   With f_r ~ c_r x^g the Perron root rho(g) of M(g) must be 1.  rho(g) is NOT monotone in g (2^-g falls, (3/2)^g rises),
   so rho(g) = 1 can have two roots or none; the table lists ALL roots on [0, 3] and the smallest one is the exponent.
   Uniform split (the truth, by the uniformity lemma): the all-ones vector is an eigenvector, rho(g) = 2^-g + (3/2)^g/3,
   roots g = 1 and g = 2.  The minimum over the 9 vertex splits is an upper bound on what any constant-split
   modulus-9 argument can prove.  (Audit 2026-09-21: the recovered draft used plain power iteration, whose one-step
   ratio oscillates on the period-3 matrices, and a bisection capped at 1.5; four of its nine entries were artifacts.)
   uniform split: roots of rho(g) = 1 on [0,3]: ['1.000000', '2.000000']
   vertex split 2->1, 8->2: smallest root g = 1.244017 (all roots on [0,3]: ['1.244017'])
   vertex split 2->1, 8->5: smallest root g = 0.774576 (all roots on [0,3]: ['0.774576'])
   vertex split 2->1, 8->8: NO root (min rho(g) on [0,3] = 1.2900 > 1; the draft printed 1.500000 = bisection cap here)
   vertex split 2->4, 8->2: NO root (min rho(g) on [0,3] = 1.1208 > 1; the draft printed 0.867659 = period-3 power-iteration artifact here)
   vertex split 2->4, 8->5: smallest root g = 0.576032 (all roots on [0,3]: ['0.576032'])
   vertex split 2->4, 8->8: NO root (min rho(g) on [0,3] = 1.3041 > 1; the draft printed 1.500000 = bisection cap here)
   vertex split 2->7, 8->2: smallest root g = 0.602605 (all roots on [0,3]: ['0.602605'])
   vertex split 2->7, 8->5: smallest root g = 0.436588 (all roots on [0,3]: ['0.436588'])
   vertex split 2->7, 8->8: NO root (min rho(g) on [0,3] = 1.2510 > 1; the draft printed 1.500000 = bisection cap here)
   minimum over the five vertex splits that have a root: g = 0.436588 at 2->7, 8->5
   comparison: 3/7 = 0.428571; the vertex minimum is close to but NOT equal to the recollected Krasikov (1989) exponent
   HEURISTIC reading: 0.4366 is the ceiling of the constant-split modulus-9 Krasikov scheme; the published bounds use
   moduli 3^k with k large and a nonlinear program, and are NOT reproduced here.
Literature (CITED): Krasikov-Lagarias, 'Bounds for the 3x+1 problem using difference inequalities', Acta Arith. 109 (2003):
   #{n <= x : n reaches 1} >= x^0.84 for large x.  Applegate-Lagarias, 'Density bounds for the 3x+1 problem', Math. Comp. 64
   (1995), part I (tree-search method, exponent 0.643) and part II (Krasikov inequalities, exponent 0.81).
   Lagarias-Weiss, 'The 3x+1 problem: two stochastic models', Ann. Appl. Probab. 2 (1992): the branching random walk model.
   UNCITED-RECOLLECTION: Krasikov (1989) exponent 3/7 (venue and page numbers not verified here); page numbers of the
   1995/2003 papers are not verified here.  Nothing beyond these statements is claimed.

==============================================================================
S4  How the three pieces fit: guarded D/E generation, leaves, and the Q1/Q2 exchange of directions
==============================================================================
Guarded inverse branches (collatz_blueprint_20260921_affine.md): D(x) = 2x, E(x) = (2x-1)/3 with guard x = 2 mod 3.
   For odd u the odd T-preimages are E o D^h (u) with h = h0 + 2j (parity forced by the guard); j -> j+1 is R(n) = 4n+1.
PROVED (checked u in {1,5,7,11}, j<=2): child(u,j) = E(D^(h0+2j)(u)), and D^h(u) = 2 mod 3 exactly for h = h0 mod 2.
Pieces: row 1 (u = 1 mod 6, h0 = 1) and row 5 (u = 5 mod 6, h0 = 0) are internal with infinite fibres whose rows
   rotate 1->5->3 from the base row of S1; row 3 (odd multiples of 3) are leaves: 3 !| 3n+1, so no odd T-preimage;
   a leaf n = 3 mod 6 is in the tree iff its parent T(n) is (T(n) is internal).  Therefore:
PROVED: Collatz (all odd n reach 1)  <=>  every u = 1 or 5 mod 6 is a node of the tree  <=>  every odd u with 3 !| u is
   child(u', j) for some node u' and some j (surjectivity of the guarded D/E closure of {1} onto the odd non-multiples of 3).
Exchange of directions (PROVED, elementary; no novelty claim: the Q2 half is the E-graph lane's Theorem 2.1):
   C  = deterministic Collatz graph (n -> n/2 for even n, n -> 3n+1 for odd n);  E = C plus the arrows n -> 3n+1 for EVEN n.
   Collatz: every n reaches 1 in C  <=>  the closure of {1} under the GUARDED inverse moves x -> 2x, x -> (x-1)/3 (x = 4 mod 6)
        is everything (reverse each arrow of C: this closure is the full inverse Collatz tree, multiples of 3 included).
   Q1 (E-graph lane's definition): every n reaches 1 in E  <=>  the closure of {1} under the UNGUARDED inverse moves x -> 2x,
        x -> (x-1)/3 (x = 1 mod 3) is everything.  Collatz implies Q1 (C is a subgraph of E); the converse is not claimed.
   Q2 (E-graph lane): 1 reaches every m with 3 !| m in E  <=>  every such m reaches 1 by the UNGUARDED inverse moves
        (reverse each arrow of E).  'Avoiding multiples of 3' is automatic: no E-arrow enters 3Z from outside (3n+1 is never
        0 mod 3, and n/2 = 0 mod 3 forces n = 0 mod 3), so no path from 1 meets a multiple of 3.  The greedy map
        G(m) = (2^k m-1)/3 with minimal k, 2^k m in {4,7} mod 9, chooses one such move; G^s(m) = 1 certifies 1 ->* m in E.
   So Collatz and Q1 are 'TO 1' statements (in C, in E) = 'FROM 1' statements for the guarded / unguarded inverse graphs,
   while Q2 is a 'FROM 1' statement in E = a 'TO 1' statement for the unguarded inverse graph; reversing arrows exchanges
   the two directions, and the relaxation C -> E adds exactly the even -> 3n+1 arrows (E-graph lane Theorem 1.1: reverse
   index j = -1 of the fibre).  Q1 and Q2 together say that the non-multiples of 3 form one strongly connected component
   of E (E-graph lane conjecture C_E).  (Audit 2026-09-21: the recovered draft wrote 'Q1 (Collatz)' for the C-statement,
   which is not the E-graph lane's Q1, and labelled its unguarded-closure count below as a Q2 illustration; corrected.)
   example m = 7: Collatz path (Q1, TO 1): [7, 22, 11, 34, 17, 52, 26, 13, 40, 20, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1]
             greedy inverse certificate (Q2): G-orbit [7, 2, 1], i.e. the E-path 1 -> 4 -> 2 -> 7 uses the even arrow 2 -> 7.
   G vs the minimal odd child m(u) of S5: on odd u they coincide iff u = 1, 2 or 8 mod 9 (KMIN = h0+1 there);
   checked for all odd u < 400.  For u = 4, 7 mod 9 G takes k = 0 (an even image (u-1)/3, not a tree node); for
   u = 5 mod 9 the minimal odd child is the leaf (2u-1)/3 and G skips to k = 3, i.e. G(u) = child(u,1).
FINITE-EXACT (X=100000, 0.0s): three closures of 1 inside [1,X]:
   (a) guarded inverse closure (Collatz in the box: forward C-orbit stays <= X): 39706 nodes (26478 not divisible by 3);
   (b) unguarded inverse closure = TO-1-in-E in the box (Q1 in the E-graph lane's sense), multiples of 3 excluded:
       27472 of the 66667 non-multiples of 3;
   (c) successor closure = FROM-1-in-E in the box (the Q2 set): 29762 of the 66667 non-multiples of 3;
   (b) and (c) intersect in 17077 nodes = the giant SCC of E|[1,X] containing 1 (E-graph lane .out S3: giant size 17077).
   (a) minus multiples of 3 is a subset of (b) (checked); none of the three is complete inside [1,X] because
   forward Collatz peaks or E-node peaks leave [1,X] (E-graph lane S3); Collatz, Q1 and Q2 are FINITE-EXACT to 10^6 there.
   smallest non-multiples of 3 missing from (a): [703, 871, 937, 1055, 1249, 1307, 1406, 1471] (Collatz peak of 703 is 250504 > X)
   smallest missing from (b): [1535, 2047, 2207, 2287, 2303, 2527, 2687, 2815] (the E-graph lane's SCC outsiders at N=10^5; their binding
       constraint is the forward peak, and all five smallest lie in (c): [True, True, True, True, True])
   smallest missing from (c), i.e. the honest Q2-in-the-box outsiders: [3281, 4010, 4739, 4922, 5306, 5468, 5711, 5954]
SCOPE: no map found from this exchange to THM-4139/THM-4146 (x^2-29/16 three-cycle) or THM-3341 (Gaussian squaring);
   the only shared structure is the mod-6/mod-9 row typing.

==============================================================================
S5  Wildcard: the minimal-child map m(u) = (2^(h0+1) u - 1)/3 (j = 0), orbits, image, survival law
==============================================================================
PROVED: m is injective (2^(h0+1) u = 2^(h0'+1) u' with u, u' odd forces h0 = h0', u = u').
PROVED: image(m) = {odd n : n = 1 mod 8} U {odd n : n = 3 mod 4} = odd n with n != 5 mod 8; the complement 5 mod 8
   is exactly R(odd) = {4n+1}, the children with j >= 1.  (u = 1 mod 3: 4u-1 = 3 mod 8 so n = 1 mod 8, and n = 1 mod 8
   gives u = (3n+1)/4 odd, = 1 mod 3; u = 2 mod 3: 2u-1 = 1 mod 4 so n = 3 mod 4, and n = 3 mod 4 gives u = (3n+1)/2 odd, = 2 mod 3.)
   checked: for odd n < 2000, n in m({odd u < 4000, 3 !| u}) iff n != 5 mod 8.
PROVED: T(m(u)) = u, so m moves one level away from the root; m(u) = (4u-1)/3 > u for u = 1 mod 3 (u > 1), m(u) = (2u-1)/3 < u for u = 2 mod 3.
   Fixed points: m(u) = u iff 3u = 2^(h0+1) u - 1 iff u = 1 (h0 = 1, 4u-1 = 3u).  m(1) = 1.
   m(u) mod 3 from u mod 9: {1,2} -> 1 (internal row 1), {4,8} -> 2 (internal row 5), {5,7} -> 0 (leaf: orbit stops).
